  "Digestive System":L("Describe how the digestive system processes food.","Digestion breaks food into smaller substances that can be absorbed. The mouth, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine and accessory organs each have roles.",["mouth → chewing and saliva","small intestine → major nutrient absorption"],["Put digestive organs in order.","State one function of the stomach."],"Explain why absorption is important."),
  "Living and Non-Living Things":L("Use life processes to distinguish living from non-living things.","Living organisms carry out processes such as nutrition, respiration, growth, excretion, response and reproduction. Non-living objects do not carry out these processes as organisms do.",["plant → living","rock → non-living"],["List three life processes.","Classify two examples."],"Explain why fire is not classified as a living organism."),
  "Ecosystem":L("Explain how organisms interact with each other and their environment.","An ecosystem includes living organisms and non-living factors interacting in an area. Food chains show energy flow from producers to consumers and decomposers.",["grass → grasshopper → frog","sunlight supports producers"],["Identify a producer in a food chain.","Name one decomposer."],"Build a four-level food chain."),
  "Force and Friction":L("Explain how forces affect motion and how friction can help or hinder movement.","A force is a push or pull. Friction opposes relative motion between surfaces. It can provide grip, but it can also cause wear and energy loss.",["brakes use friction","shoe soles need friction for grip"],["Give one useful effect of friction.","Name one force that changes motion."],"Explain how lubrication changes friction.")
